<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virgil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">70–<a href="/years/19-bce">19 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Vergilius Maro, Vergil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virgil was a Roman poet of the Augustan age, author of the Eclogues, the Georgics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, the epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s legendary Trojan origins. The Aeneid became the national poem of Rome and a school text for centuries, and its lines were quoted on coins and inscriptions. Virgil lived much of his life near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he was buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virgil.jpg" alt="Virgil" width="320" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boyd Dwyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgil_mosaic_in_the_Bardo_National_Museum_(Tunis)_(12241228546).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Biographical information about Virgil comes chiefly from ancient vitae, or lives, prefixed to commentaries on his work by Probus, Donatus and Servius. The life given by Donatus is thought to reproduce closely a lost life by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>. Some of this evidence relies on allegory and on inferences drawn from his poetry, so details of his life are considered somewhat problematic.</p><p>According to the vitae, Publius Vergilius Maro was born on 15 October 70 BCE in the village of Andes, near Mantua in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His ancestry is uncertain, and scholars have proposed Etruscan, Celtic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a>, Ligurian or Greek origins. Most sources say his father was an employee of an apparitor named Magius, whose daughter he married. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a> and Probus give his mother's name as Magia Polla. Confusion between the family name Magius and the noun magus, 'magician', probably contributed to the medieval legend that Virgil was a magician.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The commentators say Virgil began school at five and later went to Cremona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to study rhetoric, medicine and astronomy, which he abandoned for philosophy. His admiring references to Asinius Pollio and Cinna have led scholars to infer that he was for a time associated with the neoteric circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a>. According to the Catalepton, he began to write poetry while in the Epicurean school of Siro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. A group of small works attributed to him by the commentators, the Appendix Vergiliana, is considered spurious by modern scholars, with the possible exception of some short pieces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Eclogues are ten poems in dactylic hexameter, roughly modelled on the pastoral poetry of the Hellenistic poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocritus">Theocritus</a>. Eclogues 1 and 9 address the land confiscations and their effects on the Italian countryside. Eclogue 4, addressed to Asinius Pollio and called the Messianic Eclogue, uses the imagery of a golden age in connection with the birth of a child whose identity has been debated. Virgil is credited with establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> as a poetic ideal. Modern scholars largely reject attempts to draw his biography from the characters of the poems.</p><p>His works revolutionised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry almost from publication, and the Eclogues, Georgics and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> became standard school texts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> parodied the opening of the Aeneid, Lucan wrote an anti-Virgilian epic, and Statius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> engaged closely with him. Servius, a commentator of the 4th century CE, based his work on that of Donatus. Augustine of Hippo confessed that he had wept at reading the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a>. In the Divine Comedy Dante presents Virgil as his guide through Hell and most of Purgatory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virgil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Douglas’s translation of <b>Virgil</b>’s Aeneid was completed in 1513.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vergil</b>’s Aeneid gave the Imperial vision of the past, Pliny the Elder wrote a sceptical history of magic, and Apuleius’s The Golden Ass almost certainly describes the mysteries of Isis.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Virgil</b>’s Aeneid gave Rome a national epic, Horace wrote odes and satires, Ovid retold the myths of transformation in the Metamorphoses, and Livy wrote a history of Rome from its foundation.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Virgil</b> died with the poem unfinished and wished the manuscript burned; Augustus ordered his executors to disregard the wish and publish it with as few changes as possible.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Attempts to link it to Homer's Odyssey and <b>Virgil</b>'s Aeneid have persisted.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Without access to Homer, Dante took <b>Virgil</b>, Lucan, Ovid and Statius as models, Virgil above all.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Andes, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.82&amp;year=-37" aria-label="Andes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4807 571 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Virgil"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5257 841h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5257 841h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,10.82&amp;year=-37">What was here in 37 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Arms, and the man I sing, who, forc’d by fate, And haughty Juno’s unrelenting hate, Expell’d and exil’d, left the Trojan shore.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 1 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy, who had the skill to understand Nature's hid causes, and beneath his feet All terrors cast, and death's relentless doom, And the loud roar of greedy Acheron.&#8221;</p><footer>Georgics, book 2 &#183; tr. James Rhoades, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/232/pg232-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Fast flies meanwhile the irreparable hour, As point to point our charmed round we trace.&#8221;</p><footer>Georgics, book 3 &#183; tr. James Rhoades, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/232/pg232-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But, Rome, ’tis thine alone, with awful sway, To rule mankind, and make the world obey, Disposing peace and war by thy own majestic way; To tame the proud, the fetter’d slave to free: These are imperial arts, and worthy thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 6 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; spoken by Anchises to his son Aeneas in the underworld &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;An hour will come, with pleasure to relate Your sorrows past, as benefits of Fate.&#8221;</p><footer>Aeneid, book 1 &#183; tr. John Dryden, 1697 &#183; spoken by Aeneas to his shipwrecked men &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/228/pg228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
